WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.580
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
Regression was first

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:12.790
used in 1805 by Legendre, who
doesn't have a nice painting

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:14.180
of him on Wikipedia.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.510
So I picked the one
for the second person

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:20.660
to use it in 1807, Gauss.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.890
So that's more
than 200 years ago.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.650
This has been around
for a really long time.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.100
And it's still a really
popular standard approach

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.850
to machine learning today.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.580
So let's have a look.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.000
For our example, we will
be making smoothies.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.640
Every smoothie will have
one cup of plain yogurt,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.430
one secret ingredient, and
a bunch of random food dyes

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.790
so that you cannot
tell what is in there.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:47.420
I blend it up.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.380
I serve it to you.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.710
And your goal now is to
shout at me, what are

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.670
the calories in this smoothie?

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:54.170
Go.

00:00:57.155 --> 00:00:57.780
What do I hear?

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:58.864
AUDIENCE: 100.

00:00:58.864 --> 00:01:00.780
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: 100,
going once, going twice.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.430
AUDIENCE: 300.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.714
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: 300.

00:01:02.714 --> 00:01:03.570
AUDIENCE: 500.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.830
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: 500.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:10.210
Now, I'm doing a
little test here.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.500
I'm seeing who's
got the strongest

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.310
aptitude in the audience
for machine learning.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.600
Because the machine
learning attitude is not

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.530
the attitude of perfectionism,
of think carefully

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:27.380
and get the answer
right on the first go,

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.060
and don't even try it unless
it's going to be perfect.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:30.560
No.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.340
The attitude is go for
it, see what happens.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.890
Get it wrong, because
you're going to have

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.850
to do it over and over again.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.750
You're going to fail, fall
down over and over again.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.250
Pick yourself up.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.560
Try again until
finally, it works.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.980
And you have to try something
to know what to do next.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.560
And so if you're
too afraid to start,

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.300
you tend not to do well in
applied machine learning.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.437
So those of you who are brave
enough to just go for it, even

00:01:57.437 --> 00:02:00.020
though you didn't know the right
answer, yeah, that was what's

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.910
going to get me to
move to the next slide

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.240
and give you more information.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.110
And we'll be able to iterate
towards correct together.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.150
OK, so you've unlocked
the next slide.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.020
Turns out this was-- what
was the secret ingredient?

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:14.770
58 delicious grams of sardines.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.815
With 265 calories
in there, lovely.

00:02:17.815 --> 00:02:19.190
I see you don't
want to drink it.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:19.689
Fine.

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:22.676
I make you a different one.

00:02:22.676 --> 00:02:24.565
Guess the calories now.

00:02:24.565 --> 00:02:25.106
AUDIENCE: 80.

00:02:25.106 --> 00:02:25.689
AUDIENCE: 400.

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:26.896
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: 80, 400.

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:28.255
AUDIENCE: 180.

00:02:28.255 --> 00:02:29.620
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: 180.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.310
All right, I'm hearing some
more responses and rumblings.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.560
Now, let me tell you
what was in here.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.690
50 grams of not delicious
waffles that I've shown you

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.130
in the picture, no, no.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.710
The raw stuff, waffle mix.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:49.050
And this is actually in homage
to a standard hazing experience

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:52.410
that happens on the fifth
floor of the Google office

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:57.210
here in New York, which is most
new Googlers, myself included,

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.065
I've done this.

00:02:58.065 --> 00:02:59.340
You done this, Sam?

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.030
You walk up.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.625
And you see this
delicious smoothie.

00:03:01.625 --> 00:03:02.250
And you try it.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:03.910
And it is not a smoothie.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:08.209
So standard newbie
hazing experience here.

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:09.750
OK, so you do want
to drink this one.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:10.958
Fine, I make you a third one.

00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:13.110
But before I make you
guess, I'm going to give you

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.880
a little more information.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.640
This is the 16th
smoothie that I've made.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.800
And here are the 15
previous calorie amounts.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.480
Your first reaction
to this should

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.990
be not trying to read it.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:30.320
But instead, stamping your
foot and saying, I'm a human.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.000
I don't add numbers up.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.370
That's what machines are for.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.470
Indeed, correct.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.770
So the machine is going to
help us out and summarize them.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.860
And we find out that
the average is 237.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.590
And the middle thing is 240.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:50.899
Have a guess now as to
the calories of this one.

00:03:50.899 --> 00:03:52.380
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.970
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: 237
I'm hearing, 238, 240.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:58.780
Notice how your range
has narrowed a whole lot,

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.300
as I give you more information.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.560
That's going to be
key to our discussion.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.044
OK, I'll go for 237.

00:04:07.044 --> 00:04:11.650
Ah, it turns out to be 88 grams
of quiche Lorraine clocking in

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.470
at a whopping 445
calories and proving

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.600
to us that any liquid
diet is beatable.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.459
So how did we do?

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:26.770
We had an underestimate of 208
calories with that strategy.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.550
Can we do better?

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.150
Well, maybe it would
help to have the weight

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.260
of the secret ingredient.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:37.460
So what we'll do as analysts
is we'll make a little plot.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.450
And we'll have the weight from
0 to 200 grams on the x-axis

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:43.520
and calories from 0
to 500 on the y-axis.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.910
And as we look at
this, we ask ourselves,

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.230
is there anything going on?

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.074
Or is weight completely
useless here?

00:04:50.074 --> 00:04:50.740
What do you say?

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.840
Completely useless?

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:53.789
Something to it.

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:56.080
And since there's something
to it, maybe we'll go ahead

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.970
and we'll try it out.

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:59.030
Shall we?

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.790
And regression is about
putting lines through stuff.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.780
So why don't we put
a line through it.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.650
Like that line?

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.820
Good line?

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.410
We missed.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.270
Where should we put this line?

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.640
By the way, those who
forgot how lines work,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.320
here's a handy cheat sheet.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.580
The recipe, the model,
is simply intercept

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.080
plus slope times the weight.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.350
So we'll have two
numbers here in red

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:21.922
that we have to
find, or discover,

00:05:21.922 --> 00:05:23.380
that determine
where the line goes.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.300
And then we pop
the weight in there

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.705
and read the calories off of it.

00:05:26.705 --> 00:05:29.080
So that's what we're dealing
with, straightforward stuff.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.270
So you don't like my line.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:31.979
OK, fair enough.

00:05:31.979 --> 00:05:32.770
How about that one?

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:33.770
You don't like that one.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:35.140
This one?

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.520
Tough crowd.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:37.770
That one?

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:38.510
No.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:39.010
This one?

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.510
You look offended.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:47.050
Why do you look
offended by this?

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:49.780
Because let me remind you
of something real quick.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.490
This right here, this
is your previous model.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.130
And now you're offended.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:00.420
But before, you were very happy
to just guest 237 every time.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:03.390
I think 237 and 0
times the weight

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.510
means I ignore the weight
and say 237 every time.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.200
And you were so
fine to use it then.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.290
Check your feature
engineering privilege.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.600
What is feature engineering?

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.980
That's when we
create inputs that we

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.890
might use to learn from.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.940
Stuff that might
inform our solution.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:26.670
And when we have a
feature that we might use,

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.750
suddenly we can do better.

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:31.990
So that's kind of going
to be the point here.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.550
As we get information
that's going

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.674
to be relevant to
solving this thing,

00:06:35.674 --> 00:06:37.090
we expect to do
better and better.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:40.060
And it's going to be up to us
to think about what information

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.150
might be worth putting into
the system in the first place.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:45.977
OK, so let's do better,
if we can do better.

00:06:45.977 --> 00:06:48.310
We're going to find a line
that's as close to the points

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:49.780
as possible.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.020
Because you seemed offended
when I wasn't doing that.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:54.370
Fine.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.554
So we better define
a notion of an error.

00:06:56.554 --> 00:06:57.220
What's an error?

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.190
It's just the point
minus the line.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:05.890
The truth minus what we were
saying we should predict.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.800
And because we all have
type A personalities here,

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.290
we hate errors.

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:15.950
So we are going to minimize
a function of these errors.

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:18.850
And that is going
to be the score

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:20.680
for how this thing performs.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:23.950
And a good score is
to have fewer errors.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.800
And we will use the RMSE here.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.260
And this thing is
very creatively named.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:32.440
It's actually the recipe for
calculating it backwards.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:36.790
Get the error, square the
error, take the mean or average,

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:38.920
and take the square
root of that.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.740
Root mean squared
error, or RMSE.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.162
OK.

00:07:44.162 --> 00:07:45.620
Or you could just
think of the RMSE

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.320
as the calories we're
off by on average.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.150
Not technically
correct, but good enough

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:52.650
for government work.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.206
We're off by about maybe
five calories on average.

00:07:55.206 --> 00:07:55.830
And that'll do.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.950
That's fine for a morning.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.290
OK, so now we're off by 85.

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:03.720
And we want to be off by fewer.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.031
So let's see if we can just
jiggle those two red parameters

00:08:08.031 --> 00:08:09.780
there in the recipe
to make things better.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:10.738
What do you want to do?

00:08:10.738 --> 00:08:12.807
Maybe you want to
increase the slope a bit?

00:08:12.807 --> 00:08:13.890
OK, that's getting better.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:14.520
Good.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.940
How about we increase
the slope a little more?

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.450
Not so much better.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.420
Maybe now we want to
move the intercept down.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.710
So let's do that.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:27.100
And what we could do
is we could continue

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.220
tinkering with these
two numbers in red

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.309
with slow boring tinkering.

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:35.419
Or we could get on with it.

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:37.210
So what are our options?

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:39.340
Just play by hand with
those two numbers,

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:40.789
which is what we were doing.

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:42.220
Or we could use calculus.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:43.720
But who even does
calculus anymore?

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.790
So old school.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:48.760
Or we could just use an
optimization algorithm

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.960
to get us the
answer immediately.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:52.930
So what does an
optimization algorithm do?

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:56.234
It says, for this setting, you
are trying-- what's your goal--

00:08:56.234 --> 00:08:58.150
you are trying to get
this function right here

00:08:58.150 --> 00:08:59.980
to be as low as possible.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.760
Let me figure out for you
what settings, on those two

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:05.860
parameters that I'm
allowed to play with,

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:09.520
give me the best possible
performance here.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.520
And then it outputs
you that answer.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.220
And that's going to be in the
heart of every machine learning

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:16.040
algorithm.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.310
So we're going to need to
know what the scoring is,

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:19.455
what the goal is.

00:09:19.455 --> 00:09:20.830
And then it's
going to figure out

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:27.180
where to set the free parameters
in the recipe based on that.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.390
OK, great.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.370
And we have come a long way.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:31.810
I'm impressed with this.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:33.040
Nice.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:36.190
Let's look at quiche
Lorraine though.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:38.150
It's taunting us.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:42.130
We did not predict
quiche Lorraine well.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:43.170
How did we do?

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:44.000
Better.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.490
Our underestimate has gone down.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:46.720
OK.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.590
Can we do even better?

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.930
Can we?

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:52.900
Well, there ain't no such
thing as a free lunch,

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:53.710
she says at Google.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.680
To do better, we actually
need something else.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.710
So what is it going to take?

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.722
We need-- want to guess this?

00:10:05.722 --> 00:10:07.570
AUDIENCE: More
information [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:09.030
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: More
information, exactly.

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:10.210
That was very nicely put.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:12.340
Because that says
two things at once,

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:16.140
more information in the sense
of just more of the same data,

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.680
or more information in the sense
of other kinds of information

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:22.250
that might be helpful.

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:23.800
That's more features.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.975
Features in this setting refers
to variables, attributes,

00:10:28.975 --> 00:10:30.136
and other settings.

00:10:32.674 --> 00:10:33.840
So I'm going to be generous.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.615
And I'm going to give
you the fat percentage.

00:10:37.615 --> 00:10:38.740
How do you feel about that?

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:40.120
Is that going to be helpful?

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:42.250
All right, so let's see.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:44.380
So this is what we were
working with before.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:45.250
This is our form.

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:49.270
And we just had to muck about
with these two parameters here.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:52.030
And we used an optimization
algorithm to set them.

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:56.310
And notice, who here has taken
a stats course of some kind?

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.720
You probably saw regression.

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.640
It was an entirely different
story in that course,

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.340
the way that you
were going about it.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.890
It was all this other
stuff about what

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:09.480
it means and p-values
values and t-tests.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.550
None of that is relevant here.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.980
All we are trying
to do is optimize

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.920
that function to set
these two numbers.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.230
So that the difference
between the calories

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:20.850
predicted and the
actual calories

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.800
is as good as possible.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:23.440
That's it.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:24.900
That's all there is to it here.

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:28.410
Much, much simpler than
what the stats course did.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:29.230
So there we go.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:30.210
Those are our answers.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:31.510
Lovely.

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:35.590
Now, it's not going
to be a line anymore,

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:37.620
if we add fat percentage.

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:38.130
It's a bird.

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:39.600
It's a plane.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.880
Same structure, just we add
another one of these right

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:42.379
here.

00:11:42.379 --> 00:11:44.700
And now, the only pertinent
thing for you to realize

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.730
is that there are three
widgets to tinker with.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:49.620
Nice.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:52.050
And plotting it there, we
need to add a dimension.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.370
I can't make heads
or tails of this.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:57.240
So a little shout out
to better plotting.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:58.530
How about adding color?

00:11:58.530 --> 00:11:59.340
OK.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:00.390
Still not enough.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.370
Because I can't see
what's going on.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:03.495
The color is more calories.

00:12:03.495 --> 00:12:04.710
It gets redder.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.260
But I can't see what's going
on with this fat percentage

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:08.080
and weight plane.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:13.054
So OK, now I can kind
see what's going on.

00:12:13.054 --> 00:12:14.970
And if you're used to
looking at these things,

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:18.090
you kind of see the
punch line that's coming.

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:19.830
But even if you
don't see it, let's

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.540
find out what happens
if we run the algorithm.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.620
So again, our job is to
get those three numbers

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:28.020
to be as good as possible.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:29.220
We have our three options.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.710
I pick the getting on with it.

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:35.000
And so we have this
mosquito netting

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:36.950
fitted through our data for us.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:38.060
What is it trying to do?

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:41.719
Get as close to our
points as possible.

00:12:41.719 --> 00:12:43.260
And those are the
numbers that do it.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.680
Quiche Lorraine is
still taunting us.

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:47.370
How did we do?

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:51.610
We are now off by 47
calories on average.

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:55.029
Before it was 49.

00:12:55.029 --> 00:12:55.570
I don't know.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:56.860
I'm not feeling it.

00:12:56.860 --> 00:13:01.240
I had to double the number of
ingredients into my recipe.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:07.640
And all I get for that is
this measly, tiny improvement?

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:12.540
Maybe after all, this isn't such
a promising direction to go in.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.960
Maybe I would prefer
the simpler model.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:19.300
But of course, don't
worry about that too much.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:22.800
You can always try both
of them in a new data set

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:26.690
and see which one
is working better.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:30.050
So how are we doing so
far using only the mean?

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:31.990
There's our underestimate.

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:35.080
With simple linear regression,
that means one ingredient.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.977
We've improved and only
a slight improvement

00:13:37.977 --> 00:13:39.310
with multiple linear regression.

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:43.100
That means more than one
input into the model.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.130
All right, I'll take a question.

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:47.590
AUDIENCE: Can you
do a model that has

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:49.810
fat percentage times weight?

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:54.282
That's actually tells you
how much fat content it is.

00:13:54.282 --> 00:13:55.740
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
Can you do a model

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:59.400
that does fat
percentage times weight?

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:00.810
Absolutely.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:04.440
And there's a good reason
to think about doing that.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:08.370
Like, the bit about
the water amount

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.440
of the secret ingredient.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.060
We didn't even cover that.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:13.080
Right?

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:16.720
If I just dilute the
secret ingredient

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:19.710
I can mess about with
the fat percentage.

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:28.321
So-- but the reasoning--

00:14:28.321 --> 00:14:29.070
that's a good one.

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:31.500
It's a good one.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:34.830
The reasoning around
whether or not

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:39.630
you should go ahead and try
a model like that is, does it

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:41.190
take a long time to try it?

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:43.620
If no, just do it.

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:47.680
Even if you don't have a
solid of a logical argument,

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:48.776
try it anyway.

00:14:48.776 --> 00:14:50.400
You can always try
something like that.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.370
And yes, it's very, very
easy to adjust the method

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:55.140
to input something like that.

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:59.760
You just make the feature
by multiplying them first.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:01.500
And in it goes.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:02.730
Job done.

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.730
So certainly could try that.

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:10.930
OK, can we do even better,
even better than that?

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:11.470
Sure.

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:13.960
How about if I'm
really generous?

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:16.450
This is non-alcoholic.

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:19.210
And I'm going to give
you the fat in grams,

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:22.660
the carbohydrate in grams,
and the protein in grams

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:24.190
of our secret ingredient.

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:27.100
Does anyone want to spoil
the punchline for us?

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:28.197
Who knows about nutrition?

00:15:28.197 --> 00:15:28.780
Let's have it.

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:31.570
AUDIENCE: Fat times fat
plus 4 times carbohydrates

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:32.310
plus 4 times--

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:35.130
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Nine, four,
and four, the magic numbers.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:39.330
Because we as humanity have
figured out a whole lot

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:40.690
about nutrition.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:42.790
And we already know the answer.

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:44.790
Well, doesn't matter
if we know the answer.

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.190
Why don't we see if we can
recover that same answer

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:49.600
from this data set
with machine learning?

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:52.740
But yeah, let's hope
that our final answer

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:56.440
is going to look something
like that answer.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:57.660
So here we go.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.050
This thing is now
not a plane anymore.

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:02.550
It's now a hyperplane.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:05.900
So let's go ahead and plot it.

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:07.700
Let's not.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:09.930
There's no real reason
to be plotting it here.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:14.100
Because at the end of the
day, the way that we check

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:18.150
how well we're doing, how
we get that score that we're

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:23.920
optimizing, it happens the same
way no matter what is in here.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:27.040
All we need to know about is
that we take what's predicted.

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:28.970
We compare it with the truth.

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:30.700
And we see how we did.

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:34.570
And inside this thing,
however beastly it

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:39.070
is, there are some numbers that
we are allowed to tinker with.

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:40.870
And we'll use an
optimization algorithm

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:42.730
to do the tinkering for us.

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:44.650
And hopefully, some
researcher somewhere

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:48.010
has written that optimization
code, so we don't have to.

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:50.200
So that's a much better
thing to spend your time on,

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:54.490
if you're in a research
division, or research

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:57.570
in the industry, or research
division in academia.

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:58.445
Good and they have.

00:16:58.445 --> 00:17:00.070
And so we don't have
to worry about it.

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:02.153
All we need to know is
four things to tinker with.

00:17:02.153 --> 00:17:05.079
We'll still assess the
results the same way.

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:06.740
Doesn't matter what's there.

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.770
So let's get on with it and use
that optimization algorithm,

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:13.300
which we don't need
to know how it works.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.970
And there you go.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:17.470
How did we do?

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:21.849
Nine, four, four,
something like that.

00:17:21.849 --> 00:17:24.430
Looks pretty good to me.

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:26.440
FDA, by the way,
confirms what you say.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:28.480
This is a quote
from their website.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.040
Carbohydrate provides
four calories

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:32.830
per gram, protein
four, and fat nine.

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:35.590
So very good general knowledge.

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:40.240
What on earth is this 143 thing?

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:42.880
Well, we ask ourselves, if
there is no fat, no carbs,

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:46.120
and no protein, if our
secret ingredient was

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.899
water, what's left?

00:17:48.899 --> 00:17:50.690
Well, there was yogurt
in there every time.

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:53.840
Now, it turns out, our
yogurt base had 145 calories.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:55.850
We're predicting 143.

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:57.510
There's a little
bit of a mismatch.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.870
So maybe there was
some error in our data.

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:01.630
But you know what?

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:04.860
Our score is getting
pretty good, not perfect,

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:06.510
but pretty good.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:12.650
We're off by four calories on
average, when it used to be 47.

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:17.420
And this is, again, a shout
out to feature engineering.

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:22.520
The fact that you had the
knowledge about this domain

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:25.850
and realized that having
the weights in grams

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:29.120
of all of these things
was going to be useful,

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:31.790
that is the reason that
you get a model that's

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:34.100
this good in the end.

00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:36.020
It pays off.

00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:39.200
You can convert your domain
knowledge into good solutions.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:44.060
And how did we do in the end?

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:48.290
Our underestimate for quiche
Lorraine is only four calories.

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:49.110
I say, good enough.

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.040
Let's drink it.

